  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Italian stornelli improvised-couplet folk-song tradition of central Italy (Tuscany, Umbria, Lazio, Marche, Abruzzo) and adjacent regions, in which singer-poets improvise short rhymed verse-couplets (typically tripartite three-line stanzas) on assigned topics, often in competitive dialogic-exchange between two singers. Performance: unaccompanied or minimally-accompanied solo voice, with the canonical poetic-meter conventions strictly governing the improvisation. The genre survived the twentieth-century rural-urbanization period as a vestigial folk practice and entered the post-1970s Italian folk-revival under researchers Roberto Leydi and Diego Carpitella. Distinguished from broader Italian regional-folk-song by stornelli's specifically-improvisational structural framework.",
